A string's contents cannot change, but a variable can receive a different reference. Use that rule and reference assignment to trace this code:

```csharp
var original = "go";
var text = original;

for (var i = 0; i < 2; i++)
{
    text += "!";
}

Console.WriteLine(original);
Console.WriteLine(text);
```

What two lines are printed? Which string does each loop iteration construct, and why does `original` keep its initial text?

Predict the four printed Boolean values in order. Give a brief reason for each.

```csharp
string a = new string(new char[] { 'h', 'i' });
string b = new string(new char[] { 'h', 'i' });
string c = a;

Console.WriteLine(a == b);
Console.WriteLine(object.ReferenceEquals(a, b));
Console.WriteLine(object.ReferenceEquals(a, c));
Console.WriteLine(object.ReferenceEquals(
    string.Intern(a), string.Intern(b)));
